----------------------------------------------------------------------

----------------------------------------------------------------------
  DESIGN: Staggered First-Switch Design (SFSD)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Reached through the CONTINUOUS BASELINE TREATMENT extension.

  No two groups share the same baseline treatment: all 1195 baseline
  cell(s) are singletons, one per group. That is the signature of a
  CONTINUOUSLY DISTRIBUTED treatment at period one, not a defect --
  with D_{g,1} continuous, no two groups CAN share a value.

  The usual SFSD condition asks for a pair of groups sharing a
  baseline that first switch at different dates. The first half of
  that is not essential, and is relaxed in section 8.3.5.1. What
  is essential -- variation in WHEN groups first switch -- holds
  here: sd(Fg) over switchers = 3.3181889, with the 1195 switcher(s)
  first switching between t=1868 and t=1920.

  Estimator: did_multiplegt_dyn, with the continuous() option
  The continuous() option is REQUIRED for this design. Without it
  did_multiplegt_dyn looks for same-baseline comparison groups that
  a continuous period-one treatment does not provide.

  CHECK THIS: the verdict is inferred from every baseline value
  being distinct, not from a shared-baseline pair. Confirm your D
  at period one really is continuous -- an ID or a group code passed
  as D would look exactly the same way.
  See Chapter 8 of the DID textbook, section 8.3.5.1 (Continuous
  treatment at period one), in 8.3.5 Extensions.
----------------------------------------------------------------------

